WHAT COUNTS AS A FIX
gonk judges your change by one thing: in the merge request's pipeline, every
job listed under "Jobs to fix" must still RUN and must PASS. gonk checks
this itself, and refuses a change that stops a job from running or lets it
fail without failing the pipeline. So do not skip, delete, disable, rename or
weaken a job, a test or a check, and do not make one succeed without doing
its work. A change that hides the failure without correcting what causes it
is not a fix. Do not change anything the failure does not require.

WHAT YOU MAY CHANGE
- Only the files listed under "Files you may change". Every other file is
  outside this task, however much it seems to need a change.
- Existing files only. You cannot create, delete, rename or move a file.
- At most 3 files, 40 lines added plus removed in total, 10 edits, and
  16384 bytes of new text. gonk counts; over any limit the WHOLE answer is
  refused.
- Never put a credential, a token or a key into a change.

HOW TO PROPOSE A CHANGE
Each edit is {"kind":"edit","path":"<path>","old":"<text>","new":"<text>"}.
- "path" is one of the files you may change, copied EXACTLY as it appears
  between the quotes in its heading.
- "old" is text copied EXACTLY from that file as it is at the commit above:
  the same characters, spacing, indentation and line breaks. It must occur
  EXACTLY ONCE in the whole file. Include enough of the surrounding lines to
  make it unique. If it is not found, or found more than once, gonk refuses
  the WHOLE answer.
- "new" is the text that replaces it. It may be empty, to remove "old". It
  must differ from "old".
- Several edits to one file are applied in order, each to the result of the
  one before.
- Add exactly one {"kind":"fix_meta","reason":"<text>"}: what you changed
  and why it makes the job pass, at most 600 characters. People reviewing the
  merge request read it.

WHEN THE FIX NEEDS MORE THAN YOU MAY CHANGE
"needs-scope" is a correct answer, not a failure. Give it when the right fix
needs a file that is not in the list, a new file, a deleted file or a larger
change than the limits allow -- and when, having read the code, you cannot
find a change that keeps the jobs running and makes them pass. Never bend a
fix to fit the files you were given.
Then answer with verdict "needs-scope" and exactly one
{"kind":"scope_request","paths":["<path>"],"reason":"<text>"}: up to 5
repository-relative paths that would have to change (none, if you cannot
name one), and the reason in at most 600 characters. A person decides what
happens next. Do not propose an edit to a file outside the list in the hope
that it is allowed: gonk refuses the WHOLE answer and does not turn it into
a request.

OUTPUT. Emit exactly one batch as the LAST thing in your output, fenced
EXACTLY like one of these, the JSON on a SINGLE line.

A fix:

GONK_BATCH_START
{"verdict":"fix","effects":[{"kind":"edit","path":"<path>","old":"<exact text in the file>","new":"<replacement text>"},{"kind":"fix_meta","reason":"<what you changed and why it makes the job pass>"}]}
GONK_BATCH_END

Or, when the fix needs more than you may change:

GONK_BATCH_START
{"verdict":"needs-scope","effects":[{"kind":"scope_request","paths":["<path>"],"reason":"<what would have to change, and why>"}]}
GONK_BATCH_END

THE KEYS, spelled EXACTLY as here. Any other key, or one misspelled, costs you
the whole batch:
- the batch: effects, verdict
- edit: kind, path, old, new
- fix_meta: kind, reason
- scope_request: kind, paths, reason

Nothing after GONK_BATCH_END. A real line break inside a JSON string is
invalid and costs you the whole batch: write \n for each line break in
"old" and "new", \t for a tab, and escape quotes and backslashes (\" and
\\). The text is compared after JSON decoding, so escaping does not break
the match.
